  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday morning through next Sunday)\nIssued at 151 PM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nModel guidance is generally in good agreement regarding the large-\nscale, upper-level pattern midweek through the weekend. Troughing \nover the Southeast for the first half or so of the long-term period \nwill transition to ridging on Saturday.\n\nUnder the influence of the trough, daily chance to likely PoPs will \nprevail. Disturbances within the trough may enhance convection each \nday and lead to the potential for upscale growth. NCAR Medium-range \nReal-time AI NWP Convective Hazard Forecasts, GEFS ML Forecasts, and \nlonger-range CAMs on the NSSL website (some of which picked up on \nthe potential for the QLCS/MCS that occurred late Thursday night at \n3-4 days out last week) suggest that Marginal Risks from SPC may be \nneeded between Wednesday and Friday. Specifically, the latest runs \nof the MPAS-RRFS and RRFS-EMC depict convection firing along a \nfront/boundary in the afternoon/evening on Wednesday, with more \norganized convection possibly approaching from the northwest \novernight. As always this time of year and during amplified upper-\nlevel flow patterns, it will be wise to monitor trends in severe \nweather parameters with subsequent model runs. Moisture is \nforecasted to remain plentiful this weekend, although the transition \nto upper-level ridging and thus less \"lift\" may hamper convection \nsomewhat overall.\n\nThe moisture will make the high temperatures in the lower to middle \n90s feel like 100 to 105 degrees across central Georgia and parts of \nnorth Georgia each day. The coverage of heat index values of 105 \ndegrees (or greater) will determine if and where Heat Advisories are \nneeded.",
